{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 4,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.669,
            0.048,
            0.861,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Tales by Dots and Lines"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.089,
            0.847,
            0.128
          ],
          "content": "The mean of the new collection also increases by 10. Notice that the relative position of the mean stays the same."
        },
        {
          "type": "chart",
          "bbox": [
            0.219,
            0.13,
            0.79,
            0.221
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_chart_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.244,
            0.727,
            0.265
          ],
          "content": "We get the following dot plot if we reduce every value by 1."
        },
        {
          "type": "chart",
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.267,
            0.789,
            0.357
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_chart_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.37,
            0.53,
            0.39
          ],
          "content": "This can be explained using algebra —"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.389,
            0.845,
            0.429
          ],
          "content": "Suppose there are n values in the collection. Let these values be represented by $X _ { 1 } , X _ { 2 } , X _ { 3 } , \\ldots X _ { n } .$ Their average is given by —"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.392,
            0.432,
            0.608,
            0.462
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{X_{1}+X_{2}+X_{3}+\\cdots+X_{n}}{n}=a.",
          "image_source": "images/page_4_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.464,
            0.845,
            0.502
          ],
          "content": "When a fixed number, for example, 3 is added to every value in the collection, the new average becomes —"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.311,
            0.503,
            0.664,
            0.627
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{\\overbrace{x_{1}+3)_{+}(x_{2}+3)_{+}(x_{3}+3)_{+}\\cdots+(x_{n}+3)}^{n}}{n}",
          "image_source": "images/page_4_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.632,
            0.772,
            0.653
          ],
          "content": "That is, the new average is 3 more than the previous average."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.654,
            0.808,
            0.695
          ],
          "content": "Try to explain, using algebra, what the average is when a fixed number, e.g., 2 is subtracted from every value in the collection."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.7,
            0.808,
            0.742
          ],
          "content": "Try to explain this using the fair-share interpretation of average that you learnt last year."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.82,
            0.652,
            0.924,
            0.763
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_image_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.755,
            0.808,
            0.794
          ],
          "content": "？ What happens to the average if every value in the collection is doubled?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.798,
            0.847,
            0.838
          ],
          "content": "You may have guessed that the average also doubles. The following is an example with the data we saw earlier —"
        },
        {
          "type": "chart",
          "bbox": [
            0.221,
            0.84,
            0.792,
            0.939
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_chart_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.869,
            0.912,
            0.919,
            0.936
          ],
          "content": "107"
        }
      ]
    }
